course: english
lesson: 50
title: Listening: following talk, asking good questions
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Listening is more than sitting quietly: your brain has to catch the words, hold on to them and work out what they mean, which is why it is called active listening.
explain: Wait until the speaker has finished, then say the instructions back in your head: Leo started after the first of three instructions and missed the other two.
explain: Listen for the main idea (what the talk is mostly about) and for signal words like "first", "next" and "the most important thing", and for words the speaker stresses, because they show what matters.
explain: When you do not get it, ask a clear question about one part: "Which part?" helps much more than "I don't get any of it."
explain: Picture Grace whispering "I'm lowkey zoning out" ("lowkey" means a bit, or secretly): everyone drifts off sometimes, so she asks herself a question about what is being said and tunes back in.
fact: Active listening means paying attention, thinking about what is said and checking you have understood.
fact: Starting a task before all the instructions are given often leads to mistakes.
fact: Repeating what someone said in your own words is called paraphrasing, and it shows you listened.
fact: The main idea is what a talk is mostly about; the small details hang on it.
fact: Signal words like "first", "then", "finally" and "the most important thing" help listeners follow a talk.
fact: Speakers stress important words with their voice; in Australia "heaps" means "very", so "heaps important" means very important.
fact: Notes do not need full sentences: key words are enough.
fact: A summary of a news report sticks to the facts and leaves out your own opinions.
fact: A clarifying question asks about one part you did not understand, such as "What does 'erupt' mean?"
fact: Asking questions shows interest; it does not mean you are slow.
fact: Distractions, like a mate chatting, make listening harder, and moving away from them helps.
fact: Zoning out happens to everyone and is not laziness; you can refocus by asking yourself a question about the topic.
fact: "Dinnae" is Scots for "don't", and "me mate" is a Liverpool and Northern way to say "my friend".
exercise: The teacher gives three instructions, and Leo starts after the first. What happens? | He misses the other two | He finishes first and wins a prize | He hears all three of them anyway | Nothing, since only the first one matters
exercise: What should Leo do next time? | Wait for all three, then repeat them in his head | Start on the first one and guess the other two | Ask a friend to listen for him and tell him later | Write down the first one and ignore the rest
exercise: "Tell your partner what I just said," in your own words. What is this called? | Paraphrasing | Interrupting the teacher | Guessing | Copying it word for word
exercise: Kai says, "I wasn't listenin', me mate was talkin'." What would help him? | Moving away from the distraction | Blaming his mate even louder | Chatting back to his mate | Covering both ears all lesson
exercise: A mum in Sydney says, "Listen up, it's heaps important." What does "heaps important" mean? | Very important | Not important at all | A tiny bit important | Important only to her
exercise: Which is a good question about a story? | "Why did the girl hide the key?" | "Can I go to the toilet, Miss?" | "Is it nearly time for lunch now?" | "What colour is your jumper, Miss?"
exercise: What should notes about volcanoes look like? | Key words: magma, crater, erupts | Every word the teacher says, in full | One long sentence, copied out neatly | Drawings of anything that comes to mind
exercise: Grace says, "I'm lowkey zoning out." What does "lowkey" mean? | A bit, or secretly | Very loudly | Not at all | Totally and completely
exercise: How can Grace tune back in? | Ask herself a question about the topic | Close her eyes for a bit and rest | Doodle quietly until the lesson ends | Wait for the bell to ring
exercise: What is the main idea of a talk? | What it is mostly about | Its first word | The longest sentence in the talk | The funniest part of the whole talk
exercise: A child in Glasgow says, "Ah dinnae get it, Miss." What does "dinnae" mean? | Don't | Didn't eat | Did | Dinner
exercise: The teacher answers, "Which part?" Why? | A question about one part is easier to answer | She did not hear what the child said at all | She wants the child to stop asking questions | Because all the parts of a lesson are the same
exercise: Which is a signal word that shows order? | Finally | Volcano | Banana | Quietly
exercise: What should a good summary of a news report include? | The main facts, in your own words | Your opinion of the reporter's voice | Every detail, in order, word for word | Only the weather report at the end
exercise: Does asking questions mean you did not listen? | No, it shows interest and checks understanding | Yes, good listeners never need to ask anything | Yes, questions are only for children who are slow | No, but you are only allowed to ask on Fridays
exercise: A speaker says, "The MOST important thing is safety." What should you notice? | Safety is the key point | The speaker is cross with you | The word "thing" is repeated | Nothing; stressed words don't matter
test: Your mate says, "Listening's easy, you just sit there and shut up." Put him right. | It means thinking about and checking what is said | He is right: keeping silent is all it takes | It only means looking at whoever is speaking | It means remembering every word, in order
test: What would happen if you tried to write every word in your notes? | You would fall behind and miss ideas | You would catch the main idea better | The teacher would slow down for you | Your notes would come out shorter
test: Mia's summary says, "A storm hit the town, which was totally boring." What is wrong? | It adds her opinion | It is far too short | It names the town | It mentions the storm
test: A cartoon kid says, "Zoning out means you're lazy, bruv." ("Bruv" means brother or mate.) Fix it. | Everyone zones out; you can refocus | He's right; only lazy kids ever zone out | Grown-ups never zone out, only children | Once you zone out, you can't tune back in
test: Nan in Cardiff: "Listen now, cariad, first wash your hands, then lay the table, then call your dad." ("Cariad" is Welsh for love or darling.) What do you do second? | Lay the table | Wash your hands | Call Dad | Put the kettle on
test: Jayden, rough: "Oi, what'd she say? I was on me phone." What should he have done? | Put the phone away and listened | Asked again, louder, later on | Scrolled faster to finish sooner | Nothing; phones help you listen
test: The teacher talks for five minutes about volcanoes, lava and ash, with one funny story about her holiday in Italy. What was the main idea? | Volcanoes | Her holiday | The country of Italy | A funny story she told
test: Ahmed asks, "Miss, what does 'erupt' mean?" What kind of question is it? | A clarifying question | A rude question | A silly question | A trick question
test: The teacher says, "First read the page, then underline three verbs, then draw a picture." You start drawing straight away. What went wrong? | You skipped the first two steps | You did every step just right | You read too much of the page | Drawing always has to come first
test: Kayla, Gen Z: "Bestie said the trip's Friday, no wait, she said Thursday." Which day did her best friend say in the end? | Thursday | Friday | Both days | Neither day
test: Instructions: "Stand up, turn left twice, then turn right once." You start facing the board. Which way do you face at the end? | A quarter turn to the left of the board | Straight at the board again | Straight at the back wall | A quarter turn to the right of the board
exam: Identify the practice of restating, in one's own words, what a speaker has said. | Paraphrasing | Interrupting | Projecting | Debating
exam: Select the response that most accurately characterises the main idea of a talk. | That which the talk principally concerns | The opening word uttered by the speaker | The most humorous anecdote it contains | The final instruction issued
exam: It is given that a pupil has failed to understand part of an explanation. Identify the most appropriate course of action. | Ask a question about the specific part | Remain silent in the hope of eventual clarity | Declare the explanation wholly useless | Commence the task without further thought
exam: Determine which of the following ought to be excluded from a summary of a news report. | The writer's own opinions | The principal facts reported | The persons who were involved | The place where events occurred
